      <h2 id="spectrum">The Whole Spectrum</h2>

      <p>The electromagnetic spectrum begins with low-frequency radio waves, which possess the longest wavelengths — comparable to the scale of mountains — and the lowest energy. As the frequency increases and wavelengths shorten, we encounter high-frequency radio waves, microwaves, infrared radiation (which we feel as heat), the narrow band of visible light, ultraviolet radiation, X-rays, and finally gamma rays — which carry billions of times the energy of visible light and are associated with nuclear reactions.</p>

      <p>Earth's atmosphere functions as a selective shield, effectively opaque to — that is, it blocks — most electromagnetic radiation, but it is permeable through specific "spectral windows" where the air is transparent and lets light pass. The most significant openings are the optical window, allowing visible light to reach the surface, and the radio window, which permits the passage of radio waves with wavelengths ranging from approximately one centimeter to ten meters. The ozone layer high overhead blocks lethal ultraviolet, X-rays, and gamma rays, rendering these energetic bands observable only from telescopes above the atmosphere, in space.</p>

      <p>Slide the marker across the strip below — or tap it and press the ← / → keys — to sweep from long radio waves on the left all the way to gamma rays on the right. Each band lights up with both an everyday use and an astronomical one, and the lower panel shows how much of that light actually survives the trip through our air.</p>

