      <h2 id="bestiary">Telescopes, Tuned</h2>

      <p>To study the Universe, astronomers use specialized tools tuned to different wavelengths of the electromagnetic spectrum. Radio waves are captured by instruments like LOFAR and the Five-hundred-meter Aperture Spherical Telescope (FAST). For microwaves, the South Pole Telescope and the Planck satellite map the afterglow of the Big Bang. To capture infrared light, the James Webb Space Telescope observes stars and galaxies through cosmic dust. Visible light is monitored by the Keck Observatory and Hubble. In higher energies, GALEX maps the ultraviolet sky, Chandra captures X-rays from black holes, and the Fermi telescope plus the Cherenkov Telescope Array observe the extremely high-energy Universe.</p>

      <p>The gallery below lines these real instruments up by the colour of light each was built to catch — on the very same spectrum bar you just scrubbed. Hover a card (or step through with the ← / → keys) to light up the slice of spectrum that telescope covers; click any one for a full rundown — its working range, what it studies, where it sits, who built it, and what it cost.</p>

