      <h2 id="anatomy">Inside a Telescope</h2>

      <p>Every modern telescope system works as a three-part team to transform faint cosmic whispers into clear, viewable data. The process begins with a <strong>collector</strong> — usually a large, curved mirror — which acts like a giant light bucket, catching incoming rays and concentrating them onto the focal plane. At this focus plane sits the <strong>detector</strong>, typically a high-tech chip similar to the sensors in digital cameras. This chip counts photons and converts them into electrical signals. Finally, these signals are sent to a <strong>processor</strong>, where computers clean up digital noise and translate the data into the stunning images we see on our screens. This process is known as <strong>photometry</strong>.</p>

      <p>Beyond just taking beautiful pictures, telescopes can also serve as long-distance laboratories through <strong>spectroscopy</strong> by using a device called a spectrometer. By placing a high-tech prism between the collector and the detector, astronomers spread light into its component colors, creating a spectrum. This reveals the unique "fingerprints" of matter; by studying these patterns, scientists can identify exactly what a distant star is made of, how hot it is, and how fast it is moving.</p>

      <p>The true power of a telescope is defined by two qualities: <strong>resolution</strong> (the ability to see fine details) and <strong>sensitivity</strong> (the light-gathering power). A larger mirror intercepts more light, allowing us to see objects billions of times fainter than anything the human eye could spot on its own.</p>

      <p>The diagram below traces a single photon through one common design — the Cassegrain reflector, where a big main mirror bounces light off a small second mirror and out through a hole in its own centre. Drag the slider (or press ← / →) to follow that light step by step from the sky to the final picture, and flip the detector switch to see the same beam turned into either an image or a spectrum.</p>

